{
  // The scanner's NeoPixel ring as a device: 35 pixels in a circle, on the
  // stage the scanner looks render into.
  //
  // Coordinates are literal, in the same space as the obelisk device (x is
  // across, y is up, the obelisk's floor at y=0 and its top at y=42): the
  // ring is a circle of radius 3.5 centred at (3.5, 19.5), spanning the
  // sculpture's width, with its lowest pixel on the stage's origin line
  // y=16 - the height the DMX truss hangs at (config/scanner_stage.json)
  // and where a beat-synced look starts from. That is the point of it: a
  // look that travels along y starts at the foot of the obelisk, passes
  // through the ring and climbs on to the top. Pixel 0 sits at the top of
  // the circle, numbered the way the physical ring runs; the centre and
  // radius are kRing*/kStageOriginY in src/relics/scanner/scanner_patterns.h,
  // and the live game writes the same circle inline -
  // main-py/lib/jr_lib/eclipse_engine.py - so the three have to agree
  // (python/tests checks that they do).
  //
  // The viewer draws its picture from these same fields, which is why they
  // are a real circle and not a convenient line.
  //
  // Preview output: this device's frames only ever go to a screen - the real
  // ring is painted by the game (or, at a desk over ssh, by the pi's ring
  // bridge) out of the frame stream, never by a wire.

  "name": "scanner_ring",

  "coord_space": "literal",

  // What it is, for a look that treats the ring as the ring rather than as
  // part of the stage. See eio::NodeSpace in src/lib/eio/strip_projection.h.
  "space": "ring",

  "output": {
    "type": "preview",
    "fps": 30
  },

